<Base title="About">
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Software Engineering Theories is an open catalog of the fundamental scientific questions in software engineering,
      the laws and theories proposed to answer them, and the published evidence for and against each.
    </p>

    <h2>How it is organized</h2>
    <p>
      The catalog is organized by <strong>question</strong> first. Questions last longer than the answers proposed for them, several
      laws and theories often compete to answer the same question, and organizing by question shows where good explanations are still missing.
    </p>

    <h2 id="laws-and-theories">Laws vs. theories</h2>
    <div class="kind-intro">
      <div class="card">
        <h3><span class="badge law">law</span> Describes <em>what</em> happens</h3>
        <p>A regularity: “whenever X, then Y.” It predicts but does not explain. Example: Conway's Law says system structure mirrors organizational structure.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3><span class="badge theory">theory</span> Explains <em>why</em> it happens</h3>
        <p>Concepts, relationships, and a mechanism. Example: Socio-Technical Congruence explains mirroring through the cost of coordinating across technical dependencies.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      In software engineering, “law” is often a popular label rather than an established result. Some laws are empirical
      regularities, some are lessons from experience, and some are folk wisdom. Labelling something a law here says
      <em>what kind of claim it makes</em>, not how well it is supported. The evidence decides that, and a law can be refuted.
    </p>
    <p>
      The strongest knowledge combines a regularity that has been confirmed with a mechanism that has been tested. Theories can declare which laws they
      <em>explain</em>, and each such link must cite the paper that makes it. Laws with no sourced explanation are flagged
      as gaps on the <a href="../matrix/">evidence map</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Data model</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Questions</strong>: fundamental, empirically answerable problems.</li>
      <li><strong>Laws</strong>: claimed regularities (what happens).</li>
      <li><strong>Theories</strong>: proposed explanations (why it happens), optionally linked to the laws they explain.</li>
      <li><strong>Methods</strong>: research methods that produce evidence, and engineering methods derived from laws or theories.</li>
      <li><strong>Papers</strong>: published works. Each paper carries <em>evidence links</em> of the form
        “this paper <em>tests</em> law/theory X on question Q using method M, and the finding <em>supports</em> it.”</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Question, law, and theory pages are generated from those links, so nothing is attached to a question without a paper behind it.</p>

    <h2>Evidence policy</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Every paper must have a DOI or a stable URL. DOIs are checked against Crossref.</li>
      <li>Every question must be addressed by at least one paper.</li>
      <li>Every law and theory must be tested, applied, or reviewed by at least one paper beyond the one that proposed it.</li>
      <li>Every “theory explains law” link must cite a paper.</li>
      <li>Every evidence link states the method used and summarizes the finding <em>in our own words</em>.</li>
      <li>Findings are recorded as <span class="badge supports">supports</span> <span class="badge mixed">mixed</span>
        <span class="badge refutes">refutes</span>, and disconfirming evidence is just as welcome as confirming evidence.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The site build fails if any rule is broken.</p>

    <h2>Contributing</h2>
    <p>
      All content lives as plain Markdown and YAML files in the
      <a href="https://github.com/software-engineering-theories">GitHub organization</a>. To add a paper, question, law, or
      theory, open a pull request. The contributing guide explains the file formats.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
